From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] nvme: wait for the SSD after a D3 resume before resetting

The Gaokun3 SSD comes back from D3 with the PCIe link up, but its
controller is not ready for the reset nvme_resume() schedules
immediately: nvme_pci_enable() succeeds, the controller setup then fails,
and nvme_mark_namespaces_dead() takes the machine down through the
Android fail-safe in patches/others/0019.

The failed wake reached

  nvme nvme0: 8/0/0 default/read/poll queues
  Kernel panic - not syncing: nvme0: disk lost

with no L2 timeout, so the Wi-Fi WoW path is fixed and this is the
remaining SSD power-up timing. Give the drive two seconds after the
PCIe link is back before scheduling the reset. This is device-specific;
the Android unit's SSD comes back sooner and does not need it.

Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [device-specific timing]
---
 drivers/nvme/host/pci.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c b/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
--- a/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 #include <linux/blkdev.h>
 #include <linux/blk-mq-dma.h>
 #include <linux/blk-integrity.h>
+#include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/dmi.h>
 #include <linux/init.h>
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
@@ -3925,6 +3925,14 @@ static int nvme_resume(struct device *dev)
 	struct nvme_dev *ndev = pci_get_drvdata(to_pci_dev(dev));
 	struct nvme_ctrl *ctrl = &ndev->ctrl;
 
+	/*
+	 * The link is up, but this SSD needs longer before its controller
+	 * answers the reset scheduled below; without the wait
+	 * nvme_pci_enable() succeeds and the setup fails, which marks the
+	 * namespaces dead and panics (patches/others/0019).
+	 */
+	msleep(2000);
+
 	if (ndev->last_ps == U32_MAX ||
 	    nvme_set_power_state(ctrl, ndev->last_ps) != 0)
 		goto reset;
